          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.105,
            0.9,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.482,
            0.14,
            0.852,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}&=\\frac{(1-\\sin A)(1+\\sin A)}{\\cos^{2}A}=\\frac{1-\\sin^{2}A}{\\cos^{2}A}\\\\&=\\frac{\\cos^{2}A}{\\cos^{2}A}=1= RHS \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.248,
            0.647,
            0.289
          ],
          "content": "Example 11 : Prove that $\\frac{\\cot \\mathrm{A}-\\cos \\mathrm{A}}{\\cot \\mathrm{A}+\\cos \\mathrm{A}}=\\frac{\\csc \\mathrm{A}-1}{\\csc \\mathrm{A}+1}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.306,
            0.898,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}\\text { solution : LHS = \\frac{\\text { cost A - cos A}}{\\text { cost A + cos A}}=\\frac{\\frac{\\text { cos A }}{\\text { sin A }}-\\text { cos A}}{\\frac{\\text { cos A }}{\\text { sin A }}+\\text { cos A}}} \\\\=\\frac{\\text { cos A}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\text { sin A }}-1\\right)}{\\text { cos A}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\text { sin A }}+1\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\text { sin A }}-1\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\text { sin A }}+1\\right)}=\\frac{\\frac{\\text { cos } \\text { sec A }}{\\text { cos } \\text { c A }}-1}{\\frac{\\text { cos } \\text { c A }}{\\text { cos } \\text { c A }}+1}=\\text { RHS}\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.506,
            0.899,
            0.571
          ],
          "content": "Example 12 : Prove that $\\frac{\\sin \\theta - \\cos \\theta + 1}{\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta - 1} = \\frac{1}{\\sec \\theta - \\tan \\theta}$ using the identity $\\sec ^ { 2 } \\Theta = 1 + \\tan ^ { 2 } \\Theta .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.581,
            0.901,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": "Solution : Since we will apply the identity involving sec θ and tan θ, let us first convert the LHS (of the identity we need to prove) in terms of sec θ and tan θ by dividing numerator and denominator by cos θ."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.657,
            0.786,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} \\mathrm{LHS} &= \\frac{\\sin \\theta - \\cos \\theta + 1}{\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta - 1} = \\frac{\\tan \\theta - 1 + \\sec \\theta}{\\tan \\theta + 1 - \\sec \\theta} \\\\&= \\frac{(\\tan \\theta + \\sec \\theta) - 1}{(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta) + 1} = \\frac{\\{(\\tan \\theta + \\sec \\theta) - 1\\}(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta)}{\\{(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta) + 1\\}(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta)} \\\\&= \\frac{(\\tan^2 \\theta - \\sec^2 \\theta) - (\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta)}{\\{\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta + 1\\}(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta)} \\\\&= \\frac{-1 - \\tan \\theta + \\sec \\theta}{\\{\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta + 1\\}(\\tan \\theta - \\sec \\theta)}\\\\ \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_17_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
